{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# The completion verifier cannot bound a runaway: repetition is detected but nothing acts on it\n\n## The defect\n\nThe completion-verify agent step has **no bound on a runaway**. When the agent falls into a\nrepeating tool-call cycle it burns its entire step budget on redundant calls and is then **killed\nwith no verdict at all** — leaving `--force-close`, the unattested close this project forbids, as\nthe only exit. **20 of 205 epics in this store already carry a `FORCE_CLOSE` audit comment**,\nincluding `1d96` (21 descendants) and `bf50` (19), so this is the established escape hatch, not a\nhypothetical.\n\nThe step budget exists to bound exactly this failure. It does not: it is a flat ceiling that stops\nthe run *after* the waste, rather than a guard that detects the runaway and makes the agent land.\n\n### Measured: three of four real runs of this gate LOOP\n\nFour instrumented live runs, same codebase state except bf31's which predates its own fix:\n\n| run | criteria | calls | **distinct** | redundant | distinct ratio | outcome | `w24 distinct<=0.5` fires |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| FULL | 14 | 258 | **238** | 20 | **0.922** | died empty | **never** |\n| SPLIT-A | 7 | 257 | **76** | **181** | 0.296 | died empty | **call 88** |\n| SPLIT-B | 7 | 270 | 167 | 103 | 0.619 | died empty | **call 196** |\n| bf31 pre-fix | 14 | 264 | 135 | 129 | 0.511 | died empty | **call 151** |\n\nRepetition wasted **40-70% of every pathological run**. SPLIT-A is textbook: the IDENTICAL call\n`search_files(\"_loader\", \"src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler\")` repeated **144 times**, returning the\nidentical 12,554-char result each time, from call #81 to the end. SPLIT-B looped 62x on\n`search_files(\"deleted\", tests/.../test_backend_characterization.py)` -> an **honest** `(no matches)`\nthe agent simply would not accept. Only the clean FULL run was genuine breadth.\n\n### The mechanism that turns a runaway into \"no verdict\"\n\n1. `build_agent_request` (`src/rebar/llm/workflow/runs.py:327-339`) builds the `RunRequest` for every\n   workflow agent step and never sets `tool_step_limit`; the v3 step schema is closed, so\n   `completion-verification.yaml`'s `decide@then/verify` step has no way to supply one.\n2. The shipped forced-landing primitive is therefore dormant for the one step that needs it:\n   `src/rebar/llm/agent_call.py:64-73` rewrites the toolsets to\n   `.filtered(lambda ctx, _: ctx.run_step <= limit)`. Its only production setter is the bounded\n   recovery sub-call (`completion_recovery.py:471`, `_EVIDENCE_TOOL_STEPS=16`).\n3. Exhaustion raises pydantic-ai `UsageLimitExceeded` -> `interpret_failure`\n   (`structured_run.py:75-101`) raises `LLMRunnerError` -> `CompletionAgentStep.run`\n   (`completion_recovery.py:429-432`) catches only `UnretryableOutputError`, of which\n   `LLMRunnerError` is a strict **ancestor** (`errors.py:29-46`) -> `failure_diagnostic` stays\n   `None` -> `raise_completion_workflow_failure` raises a bare `LLMError` at\n   `completion_recovery.py:400`. No verdict, no `gate_error_v1` sidecar, computed diagnostic\n   discarded one frame from the surface.\n\n---\n\n## WITHDRAWN PLAN (kept visible deliberately — the wrong version is the instructive artifact)\n\n> **An earlier revision of this ticket proposed a FLAT FORCED LANDING: set\n> `tool_step_limit = max(0, request_limit - 1)` (= 239 at the default budget) unconditionally on the\n> primary verify step, so tools are withdrawn near the end of the budget and the agent must emit a\n> verdict.**\n>\n> **WHY IT WAS WITHDRAWN.** The design intent of the step budget is to bound **FAILURES** — runaway\n> tool calls under error conditions — and **NOT to cap capacity**; it was explicitly never meant to\n> restrict tool calls for large epics. A flat landing inverts that: it caps capacity for every run,\n> including healthy ones.\n>\n> The data refutes its necessity as well as its intent. The only run that was genuinely budget-bound\n> by *breadth* is the clean FULL run — 92% distinct signatures, 93.8-100% novelty in its final block.\n> Truncating that at 239 requests would land a verdict on deliberately-truncated evidence for\n> legitimate work: **a worse verdict, not a safer one**, and on a gate that SIGNS its output.\n>\n> The withdrawn plan was not wrong about the mechanism — links 1-3 above still hold, and the\n> ACTUATOR it named (withdraw tools so the agent lands) is retained. It was wrong about the\n> **trigger**: budget-proximity instead of runaway-detection.\n\n---\n\n## THE FIX: trigger the existing actuator on repetition, not on budget proximity\n\nWithdraw tools **when a runaway is detected**, so the agent lands a verdict on the evidence it has.\nHealthy breadth is never touched.\n\n### Blocker: the existing detector's headline metric is INVERTED on real data\n\n`max_consecutive_repeat` (`src/rebar/llm/usage_log.py:213-235`) counts only *adjacent identical*\nsignatures, so it is blind to any cycle of length >= 2:\n\n| trace | what it is | **`max_consecutive_repeat`** |\n|---|---|---|\n| bf31 pre-fix | a genuine **4-call cycle** repeated 31-37x | **1** |\n| FULL | **healthy** breadth | **5** |\n\n**The pathological run scores 1; the healthy run scores 5.** An actuator wired to this metric would\nfire on healthy work and miss real cycles — the exact inversion of what is wanted. It catches\nSPLIT-A's 144x 1-cycle (137) only because the degenerate 1-cycle is the single shape it can see.\nThis is also why ticket a89d-4dd2-f421-417a measured `max_consecutive_repeat=1` and concluded *\"It\nis NOT a runaway loop.\"*\n\n**This ticket therefore `depends_on` [rebar:70bc-ab05-6d4c-45a0]**, which fixes the metric. Building\nthe actuator against today's signal is the one thing this evidence forbids. Sequencing is not\noptional here.\n\n### The discriminator that works: a windowed distinct-ratio\n\nLowest distinct-ratio over any trailing window, all four runs:\n\n| window | bf31 | SPLIT-A | SPLIT-B | **FULL (healthy)** |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 16 | 0.250 | **0.062** | 0.062 | **0.750** |\n| 24 | 0.167 | **0.042** | — | **0.833** |\n| 32 | 0.125 | **0.031** | — | **0.875** |\n\n`distinct(last 24 tool calls) <= 0.50` fires on **all three** pathological runs (calls 151, 88, 196)\nand **never** on the healthy one — a ~5x margin. It is a set cardinality over a window, therefore\n**order-insensitive**, so unlike adjacency its value does not change when tool calls complete out of emission\norder (which these traces do).\n\n### Feasibility — MEASURED, not assumed (each claim has an experiment)\n\n* **A tools-withdrawn turn DOES emit a schema-valid verdict.** Experiment E1, `FunctionModel`\n  looping unconditionally, `mode=\"structured\"` / `output_schema=\"completion_verdict\"`, the real\n  runner: with `tool_step_limit=6` the tool surface offered per request went\n  `[6, 6, 6, 6, 6, 6, 0]` and the run **returned a valid `completion_verdict`** (verdict=FAIL, one\n  finding) in 7 requests. The control arm (`tool_step_limit=None`) raised `LLMRunnerError` with no\n  verdict. This retires the objection recorded on ticket 9a08-614d-a9d4-4a81, whose prototype\n  withdrew tools and found \"the exhausted agent emitted unparseable prose\": under `mode=\"structured\"`\n  the Agent is constructed with `output_type` (`structured_run.py:191-196`), so the landing turn is\n  schema-constrained.\n* **A runaway CAN be aborted mid-run, so it can be ROUTED rather than merely capped.** Experiment E5:\n  an exception raised from inside the toolset filter predicate propagates cleanly out of\n  `agent.run_sync` and survives in the `__cause__` chain (control arm, returning `False`, returns a\n  verdict as in E1). **Caveat that this plan must handle:** it currently arrives at the surface as\n  `LLMUnavailableError: the LLM provider call failed`, because `interpret_failure`'s third branch\n  (`structured_run.py:110-155`) sweeps any unrecognised exception into the provider-outage bucket. A\n  runaway misreported as a provider outage would be a NEW defect of exactly the kind this ticket\n  exists to remove, so `interpret_failure` must gain a typed branch AHEAD of that fallback.\n* `run_ctx.messages` and `run_ctx.usage.tool_calls` are both available inside the predicate (verified\n  against `pydantic_ai.tools.RunContext` fields).\n* `usage_log.failure_usage` duck-types on a plain message list (`type(m).__name__ == \"ModelResponse\"`\n  / `\"ToolCallPart\"`), so the existing signature logic runs **verbatim** on the live history.\n\n### Cost: the predicate runs ONCE PER TOOL PER STEP — the detector must not re-walk history\n\nMeasured in E5: with 6 tools registered the predicate was invoked **6 times per run step** (30 calls\nacross 5 steps), and the history it sees grows by 2 messages per step. Recomputing signatures over\nthe whole message list on every invocation is therefore O(tools x steps x history) — on a 250-request\nrun that is a self-inflicted cost on the healthy path this ticket promises not to disturb. The\ndiscriminator must be computed **at most once per `run_step`** (memoised on the step number) and read\nonly the trailing window, never the full history.\n\n### On runaway detection, ROUTE TO THE TOOL-FREE FINALIZER — do not land on the poisoned history\n\nThe earlier revision of this section said \"withdraw tools so the agent lands a verdict on the\nevidence it has\". **That is superseded.** The objection that killed the flat forced landing — *a\nworse verdict on degraded evidence, on a gate that SIGNS its output* — applies with equal force to\nlanding on a history that has just repeated one identical call 144 times (SPLIT-A). Applying that\nstandard consistently means the runaway's own context must not be the basis of a signed verdict.\n\nInstead, a detected runaway routes into the **already-shipped bounded recovery + tool-free\nfinalizer** (`completion_recovery.py:453-521`): each explicit criterion is re-evaluated in a **fresh\nhistory** (so the loop cannot persist), the evidence run converges after `_EVIDENCE_TOOL_STEPS=16`\n(post-[rebar:e1b1-859e-a7b4-48f2] that boundary steers with synthetic tool results rather than\nwithdrawing tool definitions, which 400s on Bedrock/Anthropic mid-history), and a `single_turn`,\ntool-free structured finalizer emits the `completion_verdict` from that clean evidence. This is the design ticket 9a08-614d-a9d4-4a81 shipped and gated on token exhaustion alone;\nthis ticket adds the runaway trigger it was never wired to.\n\nConcretely the actuator becomes: detect -> raise a typed runaway error -> `interpret_failure`\nclassifies it (see the caveat above) -> `CompletionAgentStep.run` (`completion_recovery.py:425-431`,\nwhich today catches only `UnretryableOutputError` under `_is_token_exhaustion`) also catches the\nrunaway error and calls the existing `_recover(...)`.\n\n**Fallback if the finalizer still yields nothing.** Recovery is not assumed to succeed: it can fail\nits own preconditions (`_validate_recovery_inputs`), exceed `_MAX_CONTEXT_CHARS`/evidence bounds, or\nthe finalizer can fail validation. Every such path already raises `CompletionRecoveryError` carrying\na bounded diagnostic, and `raise_completion_workflow_failure` (`completion_recovery.py:373-402`) then\nemits the `gate_error_v1` sidecar. So the terminal state is \"no verdict, WITH a recorded diagnostic\nnaming the runaway\" — strictly better than today's bare `LLMError` with `diagnostic = null`, and an\nAC below pins it.\n\n### The shared discriminator contract — THREE consumers, one definition\n\nThe signal must be pinned here, not left to \"e.g. a windowed distinct-ratio\", or two tickets ship\nincompatible halves. The contract:\n\n| item | value |\n|---|---|\n| summary field name | `distinct_ratio_window` |\n| window (trailing tool calls) | `24` |\n| trip condition | `distinct_ratio_window is not None and distinct_ratio_window <= 0.50` |\n| semantics | set cardinality of tool-call signatures over the trailing window, divided by the window size; order-insensitive; `None` when fewer than 24 signatures exist (insufficient sample — consumers never trip on short runs) |\n| producer | `usage_log._repetition_summary` (ticket 70bc-ab05-6d4c-45a0) |\n\nConsumers, all three of which must agree on that one field:\n1. **this ticket's actuator**, which trips on it;\n2. **[rebar:70bc-ab05-6d4c-45a0]**, which produces it (its AC currently says only \"*e.g.* a windowed\n   distinct-ratio\" and must be tightened to this exact field name, window and semantics);\n3. **ticket aec1-76e7-bb95-4c86** (usage instrumentation, already claimed and in progress), which must\n   record this field alongside `tool_calls` (its `_SHAPE_FIELDS` allowlist includes\n   `distinct_ratio_window`) so a run's repetition profile is recoverable after the fact. (Ticket d166-036c-238c-4b39 recorded the same mechanism — the `REBAR_USAGE_LOG` no-op and the\n   missing `tool_calls` field — and `aec1-76e7-bb95-4c86` is the live work ticket for it.)\n\nThe window and threshold are named constants single-sourced beside the producer, not literals buried\nin the predicate.\n\n\n### Both kill paths must be bound — as FAILURE conditions, not workload ceilings\n\n`build_usage_limits` (`structured_run.py:399-412`) installs two independent ceilings:\n\n    request_limit    = max(1, ceil(eff_max_iter / 2))   # 240 at the default max_iterations=480\n    tool_calls_limit = max(8, eff_max_iter)              # 480\n\n`run_step` increments once per **model request** (`pydantic_ai/_agent_graph.py:856`) and therefore\nbounds only the first. Measured sweep: at 3 tool calls/request a `tool_step_limit` of 239, 216 **and**\n180 all still die. The clean FULL run sat at 258/240 = **1.075**; ticket a89d-4dd2-f421-417a\nmeasured a real run at 475/240 = **1.979**, clearing the 480 ceiling by **2 calls**. A guard keyed on\n`run_step` alone passes a 1-call-per-request test and still dies in production.\n\nBecause the discriminator is computed over **tool calls**, a runaway that burns calls without\nburning requests trips the same signal — so both paths are bound without introducing any workload\nceiling.\n\n**Enforcement point (pinned from the vendored source):** `UsageLimits.check_before_tool_call`\n(`pydantic_ai/usage.py:412-419`) is called from `_agent_graph.py:1703-1706` with **projected** usage\n(`usage.tool_calls + len(calls_to_run)`) and raises **before** executing, so `usage.tool_calls` never\novershoots — the run dies instead. The guard must therefore act while headroom still remains.\n\n---\n\n## HONEST LIMITS OF THIS FIX — do not soften these\n\n**1. This does NOT make e369's full-criteria close reach a verdict.** The clean FULL run never trips\nthe discriminator, so it would still die budget-exhausted on genuine breadth. **Per the stated design\nintent that is CORRECT behaviour, not a bug to route around** — the budget bounds failures, and that\nrun contains no failure to bound.\n\n**2. The path to e369 reaching a verdict is AC-split PLUS this actuator, is two tickets, and promises\nno PASS.** Each half's genuine work (76 and 167 distinct calls) sits inside the budget once its loop\nis cut — but \"reaches a verdict\" is not \"passes\". Criterion #1's Gerrit half remains unreachable by\nany repository tool (ticket 6ec8-24c7-182b-40d0), and the flat budget still does not scale with\nclaim volume (ticket a89d-4dd2-f421-417a, ticket d59e-e042-2b94-4b70).\n\n## The AC-split fallback: tested live — both halves still died; belongs in its own ticket\n\nSplit by explicit AC index (never inferred), each half carrying the full context: 14 criteria ->\n7 + 7, context 99,347 -> 96,631 (A) / 96,086 (B) chars. The criteria partition cleanly (each is an\nindependent `- [x]` item), but **both halves still died empty**, and the per-criterion cost did not fall:\n\n| run | criteria | distinct calls | **distinct per criterion** |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| FULL | 14 | 238 | 17.0 |\n| SPLIT-A | 7 | 76 | 10.9 |\n| SPLIT-B | 7 | 167 | **23.9** |\n\nSPLIT-B spent **more** evidence per criterion than the full run; run-to-run variance (10.9-23.9)\ndominates any halving effect. Two signatures over two disjoint criteria subsets is also a **material\nchange to what the attestation means** and needs its own design. It is a capability, not this bug's\nfix — and only viable at all *with* this actuator, since both halves were killed by loops, not\nbreadth.\n\n## Measurement gap found along the way\n\nThe control-group question (\"how many requests did the epics that PASSED consume?\") **could not be\nanswered honestly**: `usage_log.record()` no-ops unless `REBAR_USAGE_LOG` is set (unset by default),\nand even then `_FIELDS` (`usage_log.py:32`) omits `tool_calls`. Filed as\nticket d166-036c-238c-4b39 — a gate whose spend cannot be measured after the fact cannot be tuned,\nthe same \"computed then discarded\" shape as ticket 8b97-a0ca-82a4-4d4e.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] a runaway (a repeating tool-call cycle) is detected DURING the run and the run is aborted at\n      that point, rather than continuing until the step budget is exhausted\n- [x] the detected runaway is routed into the shipped bounded recovery + tool-free finalizer\n      (`completion_recovery.py:453-521`), so the emitted `completion_verdict` is built from freshly\n      gathered per-criterion evidence and NOT from the history in which the agent looped\n- [x] the runaway error is classified by `interpret_failure` with its OWN typed branch placed ahead\n      of the generic `LLMUnavailableError` fallback (`structured_run.py:110-155`); a test asserts a\n      runaway is NOT reported as \"the LLM provider call failed\"\n- [x] the trigger is runaway detection, not budget proximity: a run whose trailing-window\n      distinct-ratio stays above the threshold is never aborted. Pinned by a test built from the FULL\n      run's profile (0.922 overall distinct ratio, minimum 24-window ratio 0.833) asserting the guard\n      does not fire\n- [x] the guard fires on a k-cycle with k >= 2: a test replays a 4-cycle (which scores\n      `max_consecutive_repeat == 1`) and asserts the guard fires, so the metric blind spot cannot\n      return\n- [x] both kill paths are bound as failure conditions. The RED test varies the calls-per-request\n      ratio across 1, 2 and 3 calls per request and includes a case that a `run_step`-keyed guard\n      fails to bound; a fixture pinned at 1 call/request does not satisfy this criterion\n- [x] the discriminator is computed at most ONCE per `run_step` (memoised on the step number) and\n      reads only the trailing window; a test asserts the number of signature computations is <= the\n      number of run steps, given a tool surface of 6 tools (measured: the predicate is invoked once\n      per tool per step)\n- [x] the actuator reads the field named `distinct_ratio_window` with window 24 and trip condition\n      `<= 0.50`, and window/threshold are named constants single-sourced beside the producer in\n      `usage_log`; the same field name and semantics are pinned in [rebar:70bc-ab05-6d4c-45a0]\n- [x] if recovery or the finalizer also fails to produce a verdict, the run terminates with a\n      `gate_error_v1` sidecar whose diagnostic names the runaway (`distinct_ratio_window` and the\n      top repeated signatures) — a test asserts the sidecar is emitted, so the terminal state is\n      never today's bare `LLMError` with `diagnostic = null`\n- [x] no new workload ceiling is introduced: no run is aborted merely for being large, and a test\n      drives a high-novelty run past the previous flat-landing threshold without an abort\n- [x] `tests/unit/workflow/test_completion_token_recovery.py:132`\n      (`assert runner.requests[-1].tool_step_limit is None`) still passes UNEDITED — it pins the\n      tool-free finalizer, which this change must not alter\n- [x] the full CI selection (`pytest -m \"not integration and not external\" -n 4 --dist worksteal`) is\n      green apart from the known pre-existing failure recorded on ticket 354d-c85d-6689-4302\n\n## Testing\n\n    pytest tests/unit/test_agent_call_runaway_guard_c827.py -q\n    pytest tests/unit/test_runner_pydantic_budget.py tests/unit/test_agent_call_seam.py \\\n           tests/unit/workflow/test_completion_token_recovery.py -q\n    pytest -m \"not integration and not external\" -n 4 --dist worksteal -q\n\nEvidence: `/tmp/c827-trace.jsonl` (FULL, 258 rows), `/tmp/c827-split-A.jsonl` (257),\n`/tmp/c827-split-B.jsonl` (270), `/tmp/bf31-trace-evidence.jsonl` (264), with run logs beside them."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785980329515546001,"uuid":"073c8633-6b64-41cb-a1e7-7f6f99065d93"}